======== CLAUDE.md:1306-1794 (the vacuity floor: clauses (a)-(d), the DEPENDS-ON bound, JOINT CELLS, the label clauses, the pass-11 floor, the generating set, TESTS, rule D2 (region pinned at its edge), derived inputs, the PRE-TABLE CHECK and CHANNEL line, clause (e)) ========
CLAUDE.md:1306  ## THE VACUITY FLOOR AND THE DE-ESCALATION CLAUSE (flagship adjudication, 2026-08-21)
CLAUDE.md:1307  
CLAUDE.md:1308  **"Weak earns its own round; vacuous does not get to ship" IS A RATIONALISATION UNTIL A FLOOR IS
CLAUDE.md:1309  ATTACHED** �?" the adjudicating seat said so about the main loop's own call, and it was right:
CLAUDE.md:1310  *weak* has no lower bound, so any detector that kills one mutant can claim it.
CLAUDE.md:1311  
CLAUDE.md:1312  > **THE FLOOR.** A detector is VACUOUS �?" replace it IN-ROUND �?" unless it (a) fails on the
CLAUDE.md:1313  > pre-fix build, (b) fails on a **VERBATIM REINSTATEMENT of the removed defect at EVERY fixed
CLAUDE.md:1314  > site**, and (c) fails on a **NULL MEDIUM** -- a product medium carrying none of the behaviour under
CLAUDE.md:1315  > test (the clause-(c) block below says what that is per instrument kind). Below the floor, the
CLAUDE.md:1316  > round's own claim to have "shipped a detector with the fix" is a WRONG RECORD, which the
CLAUDE.md:1317  > stopping rule already fixes in-round.
CLAUDE.md:1318  >
CLAUDE.md:1319  > ### *** CLAUSE (c) -- WHAT A NULL MEDIUM IS, AND THE TWO WAYS TO RECORD IT.  Ruled 2026-10-09 by the
CLAUDE.md:1320  > ### flagship seat (Fable 5.1, queue 7n-1 / 7n-2), confirmed by GPT-6 Astra with corrections, written
CLAUDE.md:1321  > ### here by owner ruling.  The gloss it replaces -- "a stale copy, a comment-only file -- i.e. it
CLAUDE.md:1322  > ### carries identity rows" -- is deleted, not demoted. ***
CLAUDE.md:1323  >
CLAUDE.md:1324  > **ONE criterion -- a product medium carrying none of the behaviour under test -- and its object
CLAUDE.md:1325  > differs by instrument kind:**
CLAUDE.md:1326  > * an offline differential that `#include`s the product: a COMMENT-ONLY product source, which must
CLAUDE.md:1327  >   fail to COMPILE, with a gate that reds on the compile failure (measured for `#488`:
CLAUDE.md:1328  >   `regress/artifacts/shortread/nullmedium_20261009/RECEIPT.txt`);
CLAUDE.md:1329  > * a runtime probe: an ABSENT or NON-EMULATOR binary (every session dies), or a build WITHOUT the
CLAUDE.md:1330  >   device for a device round;
CLAUDE.md:1331  > * an instruction-handler round: an IDENTITY BODY (decode and retire, write nothing) -- the
CLAUDE.md:1332  >   recommended default, two measured legs at `#527`.
CLAUDE.md:1333  >
CLAUDE.md:1334  > **THE PRE-FIX BUILD IS CLAUSE (a)'S MEDIUM AND NEVER CLAUSE (c)'S.**  It carries every behaviour of the
CLAUDE.md:1335  > product, one of them wrong, so a detector can red on it and still pass a comment-only file -- `#446`,
CLAUDE.md:1336  > the instance that created this clause.  A table that wrote "null medium" over a pre-fix leg carries a
CLAUDE.md:1337  > WRONG RECORD (L4: correct it in place, quoting what it refutes, as
CLAUDE.md:1338  > `ppcrldimimask/killtable_20260911/SCORES.txt` does), NOT a floor breach: clause (c) is then
CLAUDE.md:1339  > UNMEASURED there.  **A breach is a genuine null medium coming back GREEN, and nothing else** -- "a
CLAUDE.md:1340  > sharper medium was available" is not a floor criterion (at most clause-(e) debt).
CLAUDE.md:1341  >
CLAUDE.md:1342  > **RECORD CLAUSE (c) IN ONE OF EXACTLY TWO FORMS, never as a bare clause letter beside a row name:**
CLAUDE.md:1343  > * `(c): measured at <artifact>` -- the leg was run and its receipt is durable;
CLAUDE.md:1344  > * `(c): by construction via <row/pin>, leg not run` -- e.g. the gate pins the row count by EQUALITY
CLAUDE.md:1345  >   and a named row reds on a dead run, or the differential `#include`s the product and its gate reds
CLAUDE.md:1346  >   on a compile failure.  **A by-construction claim is checked by READING that shape; where the reading
CLAUDE.md:1347  >   fails -- no runtime gate wiring, no pin, `check_min`, a control computed over the row list
CLAUDE.md:1348  >   (`all([])` is True) -- the leg is OWED** (7n-1's two: the unwired `ppc_rldicl_probe.py` and
CLAUDE.md:1349  >   `alpha_tbi_probe.py`).  Predict a dead run's score from the source, not from the row count (Astra
CLAUDE.md:1350  >   measured luna's at 0/14 with four gate checks red, and SGI's at 6/46 because six rows test only
CLAUDE.md:1351  >   exit status).
CLAUDE.md:1352  >
CLAUDE.md:1353  > **A claimed-but-unmeasured floor clause is L4 exactly when the record asserts a MEASUREMENT or a
CLAUDE.md:1354  > NUMBER that was not made** (`#488`'s "pinned at 90": no preserved 90-row output exists, and the
CLAUDE.md:1355  > differential pins 98).  The E1 `[IDENTITY]` self-count row is detector hygiene, NOT clause (c).
CLAUDE.md:1356  >
CLAUDE.md:1357  > ### *** CLAUSE (d) �?" NULL-REPAIR RED. Adopted 2026-08-23 by OWNER RULING, on the round
CLAUDE.md:1358  > that measured its case. ***
CLAUDE.md:1359  >
CLAUDE.md:1360  > **(d) The detector must also fail when the fix is PRESENT but its REPAIR IS NULLED �?"
CLAUDE.md:1361  > the guard kept, its effect removed.**
CLAUDE.md:1362  >
CLAUDE.md:1363  > **(b) DOES NOT IMPLY (d), and #461 measured both in the same hour on the same detector:**
CLAUDE.md:1364  >
CLAUDE.md:1365  > | mutant | shape | score |
CLAUDE.md:1366  > |---|---|---|
CLAUDE.md:1367  > | `m_dmarevert` | (b): reinstate the `exit(1)` verbatim | **22/31 �?" killed** |
CLAUDE.md:1368  > | `m_align1` | (d): keep the guard, weaken `& 3` to `& 1` | **27/27 �?" ESCAPED** |
CLAUDE.md:1369  >
CLAUDE.md:1370  > The detector passed (a), (b) and (c) and was still blind to a ONE-CHARACTER edit that
CLAUDE.md:1371  > re-opened the very defect the round had just closed. (b) restores the DEFECT; (d) neuters
CLAUDE.md:1372  > the FIX. A detector can be sensitive to one and blind to the other, because they fail at
CLAUDE.md:1373  > different sites: (b) fails where the old code was, (d) fails where the new code is.
CLAUDE.md:1374  >
CLAUDE.md:1375  > **HOW TO BUILD THE (d) MUTANT �?" and it is the same discriminator the accept-row rule
CLAUDE.md:1376  > already uses.** Take each predicate the fix **DEPENDS ON** and weaken it to the
CLAUDE.md:1377  > least-restrictive form that still *reads* like the same check: a mask narrowed
CLAUDE.md:1378  > (`& 3` -> `& 1`), a condition qualified (`if (X)` -> `if (X && writeflag == MEM_WRITE)`),
CLAUDE.md:1379  > a bound relaxed. **Not deletion** �?" deletion is (b) wearing (d)'s clothes and is usually
CLAUDE.md:1380  > already covered. The test is whether a plausible future edit could produce it.
CLAUDE.md:1381  >
CLAUDE.md:1382  > **WHY THE ONE-CHARACTER FORM IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS.** `dma alias`, the row written
CLAUDE.md:1383  > specifically to pin that repair, was **STRUCTURALLY BLIND** to it: it used an ODD address,
CLAUDE.md:1384  > which both guard forms drop. *The row aimed at the defect could not see the defect return.*
CLAUDE.md:1385  > A row written from the FIX's intent tests the intent, not the boundary �?" so (d)'s mutant
CLAUDE.md:1386  > has to come from an adversary, which is exactly what the ask-again buys.
CLAUDE.md:1387  >
CLAUDE.md:1388  > **COST: one mutant per repair predicate**, the same order as the accept-row rule (#448 = 4,
CLAUDE.md:1389  > #449 = 1, #461 = 4 across two arms). It is bounded by the diff, so it cannot become #392:
CLAUDE.md:1390  > the predicates are the ones the fix ADDED, and that list is finite and visible.
CLAUDE.md:1391  >
CLAUDE.md:1392  > ### *** "THE PREDICATE THE FIX INTRODUCED" ENUMERATED THE EMPTY SET FOR MOST OF THIS
CLAUDE.md:1393  > ### QUEUE, AND NOBODY NOTICED FOR THREE ROUNDS. Corrected 2026-08-24 -- clause (d) now
CLAUDE.md:1394  > ### reads DEPENDS ON, not INTRODUCED. ***
CLAUDE.md:1395  >
CLAUDE.md:1396  > Clause (d) says *keep the guard, null its repair*. As written it enumerated only
CLAUDE.md:1397  > predicates the diff ADDS -- and **the two commonest fix shapes here add none**:
CLAUDE.md:1398  >
CLAUDE.md:1399  > * a **pure deletion** introduces no predicate at all;
CLAUDE.md:1400  > * a **DE-FATALISATION** introduces none either -- it changes what an EXISTING
CLAUDE.md:1401  >   predicate's branch does. That is most of this project's queue.
CLAUDE.md:1402  >
CLAUDE.md:1403  > So for those rounds clause (d) enumerated **nothing**, and a round could satisfy the
CLAUDE.md:1404  > floor while having built no (d) mutant whatever. `#461` measured (d) successfully only
CLAUDE.md:1405  > because its fix happened to ADD a predicate (an alignment mask) for the mutant to weaken.
CLAUDE.md:1406  >
CLAUDE.md:1407  > **Reading it as DEPENDS ON fixes both cases at once**, and it is why this now replaces a
CLAUDE.md:1408  > narrower deletion-only rule written earlier the same day: that rule was a special case of
CLAUDE.md:1409  > this gap, and two overlapping statements of one rule is the accumulation this carrier is
CLAUDE.md:1410  > supposed to refuse.
CLAUDE.md:1411  >
CLAUDE.md:1412  > **The two shapes, once the enumeration is right:**
CLAUDE.md:1413  > * **fix ADDS a predicate** -> weaken the added one (unchanged; the original rule).
CLAUDE.md:1414  > * **fix adds none** -> the (d) mutant reroutes an EXISTING guard's repair. For a
CLAUDE.md:1415  >   de-fatalisation: **swap the guard's operand for a field the guarded BODY reads.**
CLAUDE.md:1416  >   For a pure deletion: **re-introduce the removed behaviour under a DIFFERENT predicate**
CLAUDE.md:1417  >   -- not the same one widened, which is (b) wearing (d)'s clothes.
CLAUDE.md:1418  >
CLAUDE.md:1419  > *** THE MEASURED CASE, and it is why this matters rather than being tidy: ***
CLAUDE.md:1420  > `#462` de-fatalised two arms. Its guard reads `d->data`; the body it guards reads
CLAUDE.md:1421  > `d->data` AND `d->data_len`; the two are set together in one place and cleared in two
CLAUDE.md:1422  > places that touch only ONE of them. Swapping the guard's operand to `d->data_len == 0`
CLAUDE.md:1423  > **scored a CLEAN SWEEP on a 52-row detector that had just passed twelve mutants**, and
CLAUDE.md:1424  > restored a NULL-pointer read of 408 bytes. Under the old wording that was coverage debt;
CLAUDE.md:1425  > under DEPENDS ON it is a **floor breach, L2, fix in-round** -- which is what the round
CLAUDE.md:1426  > did, before the wording was corrected.
CLAUDE.md:1427  >
CLAUDE.md:1428  > #### *** THE BOUND, and it is not optional: "DEPENDS ON" INVITES TRANSITIVE DATA-FLOW
CLAUDE.md:1429  > #### CHASING, which is unbounded and is how this becomes `#392`. Named 2026-08-24 by the
CLAUDE.md:1430  > #### adjudicating seat, in the same breath as upholding the correction. ***
CLAUDE.md:1431  >
CLAUDE.md:1432  > > **DEPENDS ON enumerates exactly two things: (1) the predicates DOMINATING the diff's
CLAUDE.md:1433  > > edited lines, walked up only to the ENCLOSING FUNCTION BOUNDARY -- never into callees
CLAUDE.md:1434  > > or callers; and (2) for operand-swap forms, swaps drawn ONLY from fields the guarded
CLAUDE.md:1435  > > BODY reads (the guard-field/body-field grep the pre-table check already mandates).**
CLAUDE.md:1436  >
CLAUDE.md:1437  > That is finite, derivable from the diff by reading, and **it provably contains the
CLAUDE.md:1438  > measured case**: the `d->data == NULL` guard dominates the edited decline (clause 1), and
CLAUDE.md:1439  > `data_len` is a body-read field (clause 2), so `M11` falls out mechanically rather than
CLAUDE.md:1440  > by inspiration. It cannot become `#392` because the enumeration stays closed over the
CLAUDE.md:1441  > diff's own hunks -- the same closure the INTRODUCED wording had, without the empty-set
CLAUDE.md:1442  > defect that made INTRODUCED enumerate nothing for a de-fatalisation.
CLAUDE.md:1443  >
CLAUDE.md:1444  > #### *** JOINT CELLS -- adjudicated 2026-09-04 on `pcc2widthkills` pass 10 by the flagship seat, after
CLAUDE.md:1445  > #### a measuring seat's CONJUNCTION of two body-read counts escaped 190/190 AND THE SAME SEAT ARGUED IT
CLAUDE.md:1446  > #### WAS NOT A BREACH.  Both proposed bounds were rejected; the one adopted came from neither. ***
CLAUDE.md:1447  >
CLAUDE.md:1448  > > **DEPENDS-ON enumerates JOINT cells over the guard's operands and the body-read lvalues EXACTLY
CLAUDE.md:1449  > > WHEN THE GUARDED BODY'S OWN CODE DISTINGUISHES THEM.**  For each lvalue take the PARTITION the
CLAUDE.md:1450  > > body's own tests induce (`pending > 0` gives {0, >0}; `relative_addr == PCCTWO_T1ICR` gives
CLAUDE.md:1451  > > {T1, T2}); the enumerated cells are the CROSS-PRODUCT of those partitions -- never arbitrary
CLAUDE.md:1452  > > boolean functions of the read-set.  A mutant exempting a single cell whose reachability is
CLAUDE.md:1453  > > MEASURED is a floor breach; close the whole cross-product in ONE diff and write into the
CLAUDE.md:1454  > > contract which cells are rowed.  A value or combination the body never tests as a case
CLAUDE.md:1455  > > (`idata == k`; a byte no conditional distinguishes; both counts >= 2 where the body tests `> 0`)
CLAUDE.md:1456  > > is DISCLOSED under clause (e).
CLAUDE.md:1457  >
CLAUDE.md:1458  > **Why this form and not the two that were argued.**  The dissent's discriminator -- *a plausible
CLAUDE.md:1459  > future edit* -- failed three ways: plausibility is not derivable by reading; the mutant was written
CLAUDE.md:1460  > and measured, so it is an edit someone writes; and "single-operand is complete" is measurably
CLAUDE.md:1461  > false, since `&& idata != 2` is single-operand and escapes.  The main loop's own bound -- *pairs of
CLAUDE.md:1462  > body-read lvalues over a finite reachable space* -- was rejected as SELF-REFUTING: the counts' raw
CLAUDE.md:1463  > value space is 2^32 apiece, so "finite" does no bounding work on its motivating case (a C `int` is
CLAUDE.md:1464  > finite -- the first wording here said it was not; codex and glm caught it on pass 11); "reachable
CLAUDE.md:1465  > guest state" would force rowing
CLAUDE.md:1466  > `idata == 2` that the same record discloses; and a PAIRS cap misses `#499`'s four axes.  The
CLAUDE.md:1467  > body-tested-partition form is finite (a product of small partition counts), derivable from the
CLAUDE.md:1468  > diff by reading the body's own `if`s, and it enumerates `#499`'s width x component x direction x
CLAUDE.md:1469  > position cells from `len`, `writeflag` and `relative_addr` -- which neither argued bound reached.
CLAUDE.md:1470  >
CLAUDE.md:1471  > **The measured case.**  The TxICR body tests both pending counts at `> 0` and the label at
CLAUDE.md:1472  > `== T1ICR` / `T2ICR`, so the closure is {T2, T1} x {p1 = 0, > 0} x {p2 = 0, > 0} -- eight cells.
CLAUDE.md:1473  > Pass 9 held one open cell (both counts, T2 label: a 190/190 escape, predicted by the Opus seat
CLAUDE.md:1474  > before its build); pass 10 found the MIRROR (both counts, T1 label) and the (p2 only, T1 label)
CLAUDE.md:1475  > cell open -- the `#499` shape reproduced inside the repair for it -- and the label x p1 x p2 x IEN
CLAUDE.md:1476  > product (sixteen cells) closed in one diff of nine readbacks in existing sessions; the ICLR, INT and
CLAUDE.md:1477  > 0xe0 partitions were disclosed by count, and pass 11 then found the ICLR x pending cell open and
CLAUDE.md:1478  > in-rule (this paragraph said "eight cells ... three readbacks" until pass 11).  **The De Morgan form of the gap**, from the kimi
CLAUDE.md:1479  > substitute: `!(p1 && p2)` is `p1 == 0 || p2 == 0`; neither disjunct is true of all its lvalue's
CLAUDE.md:1480  > held values, the disjunction is true in every row, so a per-lvalue disclosure test can never fire
CLAUDE.md:1481  > on it.  That is why the per-lvalue value-domain statement, correct as far as it went, could not
CLAUDE.md:1482  > see this class at all.
CLAUDE.md:1483  >
CLAUDE.md:1484  > **Two corollaries from the same pass, both measured.**  The PRE-TABLE CHECK's SIBLING PARITY row
CLAUDE.md:1485  > compares BLOCKS; at a block that serves TWO case labels the siblings are the LABELS, and a
CLAUDE.md:1486  > parity census over the row names is what would have shown `pendboth` as the only T2-only cell
CLAUDE.md:1487  > before its table ran (Opus, pass 10).  **AND THE JOIN ITSELF, adjudicated 2026-09-09 on `sgimtedepth4dead` 7g-1 after a MEASURED total escape: a dispatch LABEL is a floor axis of an edited guard when the selector's value FLOWS INTO an lvalue the guarded body reads or writes within the enclosing function (there, `dst_mode = (dst_tlb << 10) + ...` two lines above a repair of `dst_mode |= RGBA`), or when the body re-tests it after the join (TxICR); a label that reaches the edit and touches nothing the body reads is clause (e) (the `mode & COPY` shape).  A detector rowing one label of three was swept 14/14 by `&& dst_tlb == MTE_TLB_A` while a guest-only control showed the closed defect live at the other two (`regress/artifacts/sgimtedepth4dead/killtable3_20260909/`).  The floor it adds is {labels} x the guard operand's body-tested partition -- six cells there, two rows owed -- never the labels' cross-product with everything.**  **AND A GUARD THAT SUPPRESSES A DISPATCH ADDS A THIRD DISJUNCT TO THE 2026-09-09 LABEL CLAUSE, adjudicated 2026-09-25 on `#529` by the flagship's first-hand Q3 re-ruling, with GPT-6 Astra's amendments of 2026-09-26 (`crimewidthkill/rerule_20260925/`); this text is the codex seat's 2026-09-26 rewrite of the clause (`codexfarm_20260926/codex_carrierq3.txt`, "faithful with changes"), adopted with three corrections by the flagship's final ruling of 2026-09-30 (`crimewidthkill/final529r18_20260926/fable5_final.md`, item E): a dispatch LABEL is ALSO a floor axis when the edited guard's repair bypasses that dispatch in the enclosing function.  This is an alternative to selector data flow into the guarded body or re-testing after the join; absence of those two connections does not put a qualifying suppression label in clause (e).  At CRIME the repair is `goto crime_declined`, bypassing the register switch.  The jump omits the dispatch; that omission does not make the whole repair path empty of reads, writes or tests (it reads and writes the latch).  The added axis consists of the skipped dispatch's GUEST-OBSERVABLE labels.  Cross it with the applicable code-tested predicate and state partitions under the existing bounds: the guard's dominating predicates and the jointly reachable device-state lvalues read by the fix's own repair path, as defined by pass 11 above.  This does not make every test or state in the skipped arms a floor axis; the existing partition, reachability and generating-set bounds still apply.  At `#529` the repair path tests the warning latch, so its CLEAR and SET states belong explicitly in the enumeration.  TIME and REV with the latch SET were the demonstrated holes at repair 14 -- `M3_timelatch` and `M5_revlatch` escaped at 204/204 while `M4_timeplain` was detected at 200/204 and `M6_istlatch` at 201/204 (`repair14_20260916/POSTTABLE.out`) -- and were CLOSED by repair 15 (table 14: 214/218 both; table 16: 222/226 both, `table16_20260926/POSTTABLE16.out`).  Those holes were never the whole enumeration, and detection of the plain TIME variant does not discharge the latch-conditioned variants.  The INTMASK and CONTROL-write label-swaps were measured DETECTED: table 13a `M7_intmasklatch` / `M8_ctllatch` 201/204 each, table 16 223/226 each.  An OFFSET within one label is not an axis unless the body distinguishes it: `#529`'s entry 34 (`sd +0x40`, latch CLEAR) is unrowed while its label cell is held at +0x278 -- the unrowed-slot class, clause (e).  Include only labels whose skipped arms have guest-observable effects.  CRIME's DOG arm is a bare `break;` and its `default:` body is host-debug only, so neither contributes a floor label for THIS skipped-dispatch face.  This exclusion does not establish guest equivalence for ACCEPTED accesses at those labels: CRIME's width arm supplies `+0x0c`'s observable value before `default:` prints.  DISCHARGE requires measured DETECTION by the shipped instrument of each enumerated label-swap variant, including the relevant state-conditioned variants.  Existing rows may discharge them; predictions or aggregate detection do not substitute for the individual measurements.  Table 11's K2 PER-ROW prediction error is the recorded caution.  The added label enumeration is bounded to the ONE skipped dispatch in the enclosing function; it does not recurse into further dispatches or dispatches in callees.  MINTED FROM ONE DEVICE: on first reuse, check that the skipped-label enumeration and its bounded predicate/state product fit the new device.**  And the measuring seat's own bound -- the cross-product
CLAUDE.md:1488  > of the value-CLASSES the rows establish, with the dispatch axes as full axes, demand
CLAUDE.md:1489  > INCREMENTAL -- agrees with the body-tested form on every cell that mattered here and differs on
CLAUDE.md:1490  > where the classes come from (the rows, or the body's tests); that residual difference is
CLAUDE.md:1491  > carried to the next pass as a named question rather than settled by the author.
CLAUDE.md:1492  >
CLAUDE.md:1493  > #### *** THE LETTER OVER-DEMANDED, AND ITS OWN AUTHOR BOUNDED IT ONE PASS LATER (pass 11, 2026-09-04). ***
CLAUDE.md:1494  >
CLAUDE.md:1495  > Read literally, "the cross-product of the body-tested partitions" demanded ALL 128 cells at the TxICR
CLAUDE.md:1496  > arm -- the body also tests `idata & IEN`, `idata & ICLR`, the register's INT bit and `idata & 0xe0` --
CLAUDE.md:1497  > and it compounds as 2^k in the `if`-count across twelve arms: the `#392` shape wearing the rule's
CLAUDE.md:1498  > clothes.  Three seats said the letter demands the remainder; the flagship seat, asked to be harder on
CLAUDE.md:1499  > its own rule than on anyone, ruled AGAINST the letter:
CLAUDE.md:1500  >
CLAUDE.md:1501  > > **The rule ENUMERATES joint cells wherever the body distinguishes them; the FLOOR -- the cells a row
CLAUDE.md:1502  > > is OWED for -- is the cross-product of the guard's dominating predicates with the device-STATE lvalues
CLAUDE.md:1503  > > the fix's own repair path reads that are JOINTLY REACHABLE through its own data flow.**  The bits of
CLAUDE.md:1504  > > the single value being written are single-operand MARGINALS, not floor; joint cells outside the floor
CLAUDE.md:1505  > > are clause-(e) coverage debt, subject to the one ask-again.  A filter may reject or discharge a cell
CLAUDE.md:1506  > > but may demand a new row only for a floor cell -- so it cannot grow unbounded.
CLAUDE.md:1507  >
CLAUDE.md:1508  > At TxICR the floor is {label} x {p1} x {p2}: one `pcc_timer_tick` callback increments both counts and
CLAUDE.md:1509  > `reassert_interrupts` reads both, so the pair is a joint input of the model.  Eight cells, all rowed.
CLAUDE.md:1510  >
CLAUDE.md:1511  > #### *** THE FLOOR INCLUDES THE SINGLE-AXIS GENERATING SET, AND "DERIVED" STATE IS NOT DISPOSABLE.
CLAUDE.md:1512  > #### Adjudicated 2026-09-04 by the fix review (Fable 5.1 adjudicating; GPT-6 Astra at max dissenting on the
CLAUDE.md:1513  > #### bound and supplying the two findings that made the amendment measurable). ***
CLAUDE.md:1514  >
CLAUDE.md:1515  > > **At each floor cell, every idata bit the guarded body TESTS is written both clear and set.**  The bare
CLAUDE.md:1516  > > state floor above does not catch `M_ICLR` -- an idata-bit x state joint -- and would grade the pass-11
CLAUDE.md:1517  > > escape as debt while the CHANGELOG grades it L2; the generating set is what makes the two consistent.
CLAUDE.md:1518  > > Multi-axis idata joints stay clause-(e) debt.  **The codex seat adopts the Opus strength-t covering
CLAUDE.md:1519  > > array instead** (its `t` = the body's longest conjunction) and is RECORDED, not adopted -- for COST (seven
CLAUDE.md:1520  > > binary axes at TxICR make a strength-4 array tens of pty cells per arm: the 2^k growth by another door) and
CLAUDE.md:1521  > > because a covering array certifies that a tuple OCCURS, not that an observable differs, so it cannot be the
CLAUDE.md:1522  > > floor without the CHANNEL obligation doing the work.  *This clause first said "`t` is a judgment input";
CLAUDE.md:1523  > > the array's own author refuted that on pass 13 -- `t` is read off the body -- and supplied the two grounds.*
CLAUDE.md:1524  >
CLAUDE.md:1525  > > #### *** "TESTS" MEANS **MASKS OR COMPARES AS A CASE**, NOT "DEPENDS ON".  Pinned 2026-09-25 by the flagship
CLAUDE.md:1526  > > #### ruling on `#549` v6's adjudication pass (D1), after ONE true quotation of the sentence above carried TWO
CLAUDE.md:1527  > > #### readings through two passes. ***
CLAUDE.md:1528  > >
CLAUDE.md:1529  > > `idata & ICLR` tests a bit.  **A RELATIONAL compare such as `idata >= 8` tests NO bit: its generating set is its
CLAUDE.md:1530  > > three regions, below / at / past the bound** -- `#548`'s P_win ruling -- so at each floor cell the obligation is
CLAUDE.md:1531  > > those three regions, never "every bit set alone".  Crossing a floor cell with a single bit of a compared value is
CLAUDE.md:1532  > > the two-axis joint a probe's NOT CLAIMED (0) discloses; `#549`'s latch x bit was graded that way even though its
CLAUDE.md:1533  > > measured consequence above bit 32 was the round's own defect (silent truncation onto a legal port), because
CLAUDE.md:1534  > > grading by consequence would reopen `#548` (`pass_v6/fable5_f2_v6.md`).  **Four packet-fed seats read the
CLAUDE.md:1535  > > sentence without its bounds -- my brief quoted it alone -- and all four graded the joint FLOOR:** a rule's
CLAUDE.md:1536  > > bounds travel with its quotation, or the quotation is a different rule.
CLAUDE.md:1537  >
CLAUDE.md:1538  > > #### *** AND A REGION IS PINNED AT ITS EDGE, NOT ANYWHERE INSIDE IT.  Rule D2, adopted 2026-10-09 by OWNER RULING,
CLAUDE.md:1539  > > #### on a case Fable 5.1 and Opus 5.5 measured independently on `#563`; `asiceventaddr` needed it the same day. ***
CLAUDE.md:1540  > >
CLAUDE.md:1541  > > For a relational compare against a constant bound B that a fix DEPENDS ON (`X > B`, `e < 96`), each region of its
CLAUDE.md:1542  > > generating set is discharged by the row that varies under the LEAST-OBSERVABLE non-equivalent move of B: **X = B+1
CLAUDE.md:1543  > > for the region past the bound and X = B-1 for the region below it** (the "at" member is dead where the branch maps B
CLAUDE.md:1544  > > to itself).  **A far row proves the branch EXISTS and discharges the BRANCH partition; it never pins the BOUND.**
CLAUDE.md:1545  > > Measured on `#473`'s shipped WDC detector: its rows at 68928 and 4998 let `> 32769` and `> 32766` at each clamp --
CLAUDE.md:1546  > > one-token bound moves -- pass all 128 rows, while rows at 32769 and 32767 red each one
CLAUDE.md:1547  > > (`regress/artifacts/wdc473floor/pass1_20261009/fable5_meas/`).  **Bound: at most two rows per compare the fix
CLAUDE.md:1548  > > depends on -- the compare's own two edges.**  It adds no axis; it moves the rows a region already owes to where the
CLAUDE.md:1549  > > bound is observable, so it cannot become `#392`.  It is the accept-row finding -- *a single accept row pins exactly
CLAUDE.md:1550  > > ONE bound, the one it is near* -- stated for compares.
CLAUDE.md:1551  >
CLAUDE.md:1552  > **The two measured consequences, both found by a seat reading the ENUMERATION against the handler rather
CLAUDE.md:1553  > than the tables against the enumeration:**
CLAUDE.md:1554  >
CLAUDE.md:1555  > * **The enumeration listed five arms as having NO `if` and each has one** -- VECBASE, SCCICR, SCCTX, SCCRX,
CLAUDE.md:1556  >   SCSIICR test their HUH? bits on the composed idata.  The record then said every wide value the rows drove
CLAUDE.md:1557  >   at them had those bits SET, so `&& (idata & ~mask)` would sweep the table.  **Measured FALSE the same
CLAUDE.md:1558  >   evening (part 13, M_HUHV 249/251):** two announce-count rows (`latch all zero`, `latch all one`) already
CLAUDE.md:1559  >   drove HUH?-clear values (0x0000 alone at VECBASE, whose mask is 0xf0; the six `latch all*` rows at the
CLAUDE.md:1560  >   0x1f/0xdf arms -- 244/251 there), and their counts drop when the write lands -- the partition was HELD by a channel
CLAUDE.md:1561  >   the enumeration did not know it had.  *An enumeration is checked against the BODY and against the ROWS
CLAUDE.md:1562  >   before a breach is declared; "it would sweep the table" is a MEASUREMENT, never an inference -- and the
CLAUDE.md:1563  >   seat that raised the finding had said so ("existing rows may discharge their partitions; the enumeration
CLAUDE.md:1564  >   must actually show that") while the main loop wrote the sweep as a fact.*
CLAUDE.md:1565  > * **The INT bit was dismissed as "derived from the count" and it is not**: `reassert_interrupts()` only SETS
CLAUDE.md:1566  >   it, the body clears it only on ICLR, and an IEN-clear byte write zeroes the count -- so (INT set, count 0)
CLAUDE.md:1567  >   is a reachable joint state of two body-read lvalues that no row held.  **Reachability of a joint cell is
CLAUDE.md:1568  >   derived from the WRITERS of each lvalue, never from a steady-state invariant** -- "INT follows the count"
CLAUDE.md:1569  >   was true between ticks and false across the one write the guest driver makes to disable a timer.
CLAUDE.md:1570  >
CLAUDE.md:1571  > *Both went to pass 13 as floor cells (nine rows, seven mutants).  The fix itself was unchanged by the review,
CLAUDE.md:1572  > which is the finding worth keeping about the review: eleven passes on the detector and none on the fix, and
CLAUDE.md:1573  > the first pass on the fix approved it block by block while finding the detector's enumeration wrong.*
CLAUDE.md:1574  >
CLAUDE.md:1575  > **What the debt costs when it has a measured warrant, and it is cheap:** the measuring seat built the
CLAUDE.md:1576  > disclosed `&& !(p2 && (idata & ICLR))` and it escaped the 203-row instrument, as three reading seats
CLAUDE.md:1577  > had predicted -- so it was repaired in the GENERATING-SET form (grok), and GRADED **L2**, a floor breach
CLAUDE.md:1578  > repaired in-round, by the pass-11 disposition in the CHANGELOG (the cell was inside the closure by the
CLAUDE.md:1579  > round's own pass-3 sentence).  *This clause said "L1 construction" until 2026-09-04, when the GPT-6 Astra
CLAUDE.md:1580  > fix review found the carrier and the CHANGELOG grading ONE finding two ways; the CHANGELOG's is the ruling
CLAUDE.md:1581  > and this is the correction.*  The form: at every
CLAUDE.md:1582  > rowed pending cell, write each tested idata bit both clear and set, so any single-axis extra conjunct on
CLAUDE.md:1583  > one of them lands a write and reds a readback.  Twelve wide writes appended to three existing sessions;
CLAUDE.md:1584  > no new session, no new load sensitivity.  Multi-axis idata joints stay disclosed; INT-clear-with-a-count-
CLAUDE.md:1585  > pending is disclosed as UNREACHABLE with its derivation (the tick increments only under IEN and reassert
CLAUDE.md:1586  > sets INT before any guard runs), which is the honest form of a cell nobody rows.
CLAUDE.md:1587  >
CLAUDE.md:1588  > *Two records lessons from the same pass.  A CORRECTION IS NOT SAFER THAN THE THING IT CORRECTS: pass 10
CLAUDE.md:1589  > accepted "the store mask is a writer of ICLR" and made a true sentence false -- `0x17 & 0x08 == 0`, one
CLAUDE.md:1590  > AND that nobody ran.  And a PREDICTION COPIED INTO A MEASURED COLUMN is the frozen-number class in a new
CLAUDE.md:1591  > coat: a table said (0x13) where the log said 0x33; the score matched by coincidence and the value was a
CLAUDE.md:1592  > wrong record until a seat read the log.*
CLAUDE.md:1593  >
CLAUDE.md:1594  > *Count the arguments, not the seats: seven of eight landed seats put the pair inside the closure,
CLAUDE.md:1595  > and the one that did not (minimax) was reading the CONTRACT's per-lvalue wording rather than the
CLAUDE.md:1596  > clause -- the wording the discriminator sentence now replaces.*
CLAUDE.md:1597  >
CLAUDE.md:1598  > #### *** A DERIVED INPUT IS GRADED BY WHAT THE BODY TESTS ON IT, NEVER BY THE FLOW THAT REACHES IT.
CLAUDE.md:1599  > #### Ruled 2026-10-09 (Fable 5.1, queue 7n-5; GPT-6 Astra confirmed with corrections), written by
CLAUDE.md:1600  > #### owner ruling. ***
CLAUDE.md:1601  >
CLAUDE.md:1602  > A local computed from the function's inputs and feeding a body-read operand gets the partition the
CLAUDE.md:1603  > body induces on IT: `#519`'s `tile_nr` -- tested `>= 256` on the tiled branch and `>= 32` on the
CLAUDE.md:1604  > LIN_A branch, never for zero -- is clause (e), not floor, and so is its tile-index escape family.
CLAUDE.md:1605  > Data flow alone is the transitive chasing the DEPENDS-ON bound forbids; a dispatch LABEL is a floor
CLAUDE.md:1606  > axis only by the label clauses above.  **"Closable by a finite row set" is NOT the discriminator**: it
CLAUDE.md:1607  > is the 09-04 finiteness bound under a new name, and every single slice is one-row closable while the
CLAUDE.md:1608  > family never is -- so it separated nothing, and one species was graded two ways by it.
CLAUDE.md:1609  >
CLAUDE.md:1610  > **EXHAUSTED / SAMPLED is adopted as clause-(e) disclosure vocabulary**: EXHAUSTED = every cell of the
CLAUDE.md:1611  > BODY-TESTED partition of that lvalue is rowed (never "every value in the domain"); SAMPLED = driven at
CLAUDE.md:1612  > two or more values, with the untested slices and their derived or measured sweeps named.  A
CLAUDE.md:1613  > disclosure vocabulary can never demand a row.  Its landing spot is the PRE-TABLE CHECK's GUARD FIELD
CLAUDE.md:1614  > vs BODY FIELD row below, which now also records the partition the body induces on each lvalue.
CLAUDE.md:1615  
CLAUDE.md:1616  > ### *** THE PRE-TABLE CHECK -- three greps, ~30 min, and it protects the ASK-AGAIN'S
CLAUDE.md:1617  > ### CAP rather than the clock. Adopted 2026-08-24; two seats derived it independently. ***
CLAUDE.md:1618  >
CLAUDE.md:1619  > > ## *** MANDATORY as of 2026-08-28, by flagship adjudication. It was RECOMMENDED prose for
CLAUDE.md:1620  > > ## four days and TWO rounds skipped it -- which is this file's own structure-first trigger,
CLAUDE.md:1621  > > ## met exactly. ***
CLAUDE.md:1622  > >
CLAUDE.md:1623  > > **SCOPE, and this is what answers the cost objection: it binds EXACTLY the population the
CLAUDE.md:1624  > > vacuity floor already binds** -- any round shipping or EXTENDING a kill-table-backed
CLAUDE.md:1625  > > detector. **Not every round.** Records-only, documented-only and table-less harness rounds
CLAUDE.md:1626  > > are untouched.
CLAUDE.md:1627  > >
CLAUDE.md:1628  > > **FORM: recorded OUTPUT, never assertion** -- the two-part-clearance precedent, *run it, do
CLAUDE.md:1629  > > not assert it*. One `PRETABLE.out` in the round's kill-table artifacts dir (or a probe-header
CLAUDE.md:1630  > > section), **written BEFORE table 1 runs so its own mtime proves the order** -- the same
CLAUDE.md:1631  > > order discriminator the oracle-correction rule uses. It carries: the sibling arms compared
CLAUDE.md:1632  > > and any asymmetry; each enumerated claim sentence mapped to a row or an owed sentence; the
CLAUDE.md:1633  > > guard-field/body-field lists and any disagreeing-state row owed; and per row, the mutant it
CLAUDE.md:1634  > > kills and the observable that differs.
CLAUDE.md:1635  > >
CLAUDE.md:1636  > > **Any check may discharge `N/A` WITH ITS REASON RECORDED. An unrun check and an inapplicable
CLAUDE.md:1637  > > one must not render the same** -- this project's oldest rule about blanks.
CLAUDE.md:1638  > >
CLAUDE.md:1639  > > ### *** AND A THIRD FAILURE MODE, MEASURED 2026-08-28: THE BLOCK WAS RUN, ITS ROWS WERE
CLAUDE.md:1640  > > ### PRESCRIBED, AND THEY WERE LOST WHEN THE ROW LIST WAS REWRITTEN THREE HOURS LATER. ***
CLAUDE.md:1641  > >
CLAUDE.md:1642  > > `#490`'s `PRETABLE.out` named two mutants at **16:05** -- a latch-bit collision and a brace
CLAUDE.md:1643  > > slip -- and prescribed a row for each (`crossbit`, `latch cond`). At **18:45** a nine-seat
CLAUDE.md:1644  > > panel found a DIFFERENT defect (the row set was site-asymmetric), the addition list was
CLAUDE.md:1645  > > rewritten to carry the panel's findings, and **both earlier rows silently vanished.** The
CLAUDE.md:1646  > > measuring seat then BUILT both mutants and both escaped the committed 13-row detector --
CLAUDE.md:1647  > > two clause-(d) floor breaches that the round's own artifact had predicted and specified.
CLAUDE.md:1648  > >
CLAUDE.md:1649  > > **Nothing was forgotten in the sense of never known.** The knowledge sat in the file that
CLAUDE.md:1650  > > exists precisely to be consulted at row-writing time, and did not survive a rewrite in a
CLAUDE.md:1651  > > DIFFERENT artifact. The new list was not careless -- it correctly added everything the
CLAUDE.md:1652  > > panel had just found.
CLAUDE.md:1653  > >
CLAUDE.md:1654  > > > **A ROW LIST REWRITTEN FROM A PANEL'S FINDINGS IS DIFFED AGAINST THE PRE-TABLE BLOCK'S
CLAUDE.md:1655  > > > OWN PRESCRIBED ROWS, NOT MERELY EXTENDED WITH THE NEW ONES.** The block PRESCRIBES rows;
CLAUDE.md:1656  > > > a later list that does not contain them has dropped an obligation, and the diff is the
CLAUDE.md:1657  > > > only thing that says so.
CLAUDE.md:1658  > >
CLAUDE.md:1659  > > *This is the block's own lesson turned on the block: it exists because a recommended check
CLAUDE.md:1660  > > is memory and memory does not fire. Running it once does not make its OUTPUT durable --
CLAUDE.md:1661  > > the output has to be carried forward, and nothing was checking that it was.*
CLAUDE.md:1662  > >
CLAUDE.md:1663  > > **AND THE GUARD-FIELD CHECK COULD NOT HAVE CAUGHT EITHER**, which is worth knowing before
CLAUDE.md:1664  > > relying on it: it asks where the guard field and the body field DIFFER. For the latch
CLAUDE.md:1665  > > collision they are the SAME field (`d->warned`). **For a shared bitfield the question is
CLAUDE.md:1666  > > *"who else writes this field, and can they set my bit?"*** -- measured here as
CLAUDE.md:1667  > > `grep -c 'd->warned |='` = 8 sites, **6 of them inside `DEVICE_ACCESS`**, so most bits are
CLAUDE.md:1668  > > set by ordinary guest register reads and a collision would be silenced by benign traffic.
CLAUDE.md:1669  >
CLAUDE.md:1670  > > **THE TRIGGER WAS MET TWICE IN FOUR DAYS, and the value at each was specific rather than
CLAUDE.md:1671  > > general:** at `#485` the GUARD-FIELD grep enumerates `D1..D5` mechanically, because the
CLAUDE.md:1672  > > `default:` arm's `fatal()` arguments ARE the guarded body's field reads; at `#489` the
CLAUDE.md:1673  > > CHANNEL line names the one shape all four escapes shared -- **and the probe header carried
CLAUDE.md:1674  > > the governing sentence before three of the four were found.** Knowledge present, unconsulted
CLAUDE.md:1675  > > at row-writing time. *A recommended check is memory, and this file's finding is that memory
CLAUDE.md:1676  > > does not fire.*
CLAUDE.md:1677  > >
CLAUDE.md:1678  > > **ENFORCEMENT is existence-only and FILED, not built:** a commit adding a killtable artifacts
CLAUDE.md:1679  > > dir must add `PRETABLE.out` beside it, forward-only from a cutoff. That is a tooling change
CLAUDE.md:1680  > > owing two negative controls, so it ships as its own harness round rather than as a rider.
CLAUDE.md:1681  > > Until it lands the mandate lives HERE, in lifecycle steps 4-5, and in `/round`.
CLAUDE.md:1682  > >
CLAUDE.md:1683  > > *** AND THE FIRST MANDATED APPLICATION MUST **CHECK** THE CHANNEL LINE, NOT MERELY OBEY IT.
CLAUDE.md:1684  > > *** CHANNEL is one day old, and this file's freshly-minted-exemplar rule says a new rule's
CLAUDE.md:1685  > > example is a HYPOTHESIS. Its claim is that a row's channel-under-its-mutant is knowable at
CLAUDE.md:1686  > > WRITING time, before any table runs. One sentence in the first `PRETABLE.out` says whether
CLAUDE.md:1687  > > that held.
CLAUDE.md:1688  > >
CLAUDE.md:1689  > > *Provenance note, because the ruling corrected my own citation: the twice-skipped rounds are
CLAUDE.md:1690  > > `#485` (wdcnoirq) and `#489`, **not `#486`** -- `#486` is wdcfloodstale's non-discriminating
CLAUDE.md:1691  > > gate row. The substance was intact and the number was wrong, and a wrong number in a
CLAUDE.md:1692  > > mandate's provenance gets reused.*
CLAUDE.md:1693  >
CLAUDE.md:1694  > *** THE DIAGNOSIS THAT PROMPTED IT WAS WRONG, AND THE CORRECTION IS THE USEFUL PART. ***
CLAUDE.md:1695  > `#462` ran a 12-mutant table clean, then one capped ask-again found escapes needing five
CLAUDE.md:1696  > rows -- and adding rows changed other mutants' scores, forcing a **second four-hour
CLAUDE.md:1697  > table**. The adjudicator blamed obligations caught too late. **A seat then checked the
CLAUDE.md:1698  > preserved logs and refuted that: both "late" rows were in the FIRST table already.** They
CLAUDE.md:1699  > cost nothing. What cost four hours was that **the probe cannot score one row** -- 39
CLAUDE.md:1700  > `session()` calls, no row filter -- so re-measuring five rows meant re-running everything.
CLAUDE.md:1701  >
CLAUDE.md:1702  > **So the checklist does NOT save the table.** Its real value is narrower and it is worth
CLAUDE.md:1703  > having: **the ask-again is capped at ONE, and every hole it spends itself naming is a
CLAUDE.md:1704  > query not spent on coverage debt.** A detector arriving with visible holes wastes the cap.
CLAUDE.md:1705  >
CLAUDE.md:1706  > | check | how | catches |
CLAUDE.md:1707  > |---|---|---|
CLAUDE.md:1708  > | **SIBLING PARITY** | for each arm the diff de-fatalises, compare its observable channels against its nearest sibling arm. An asymmetry is a row you owe or a sentence you owe | a de-fatalised arm with NO completion-event row while its sibling had one |
CLAUDE.md:1709  > | **CLAIM -> ROW** | every ADDED comment sentence asserting an OBSERVABLE CONSEQUENCE owes a row or an explicit no-row sentence. Claim verbs (*observes, controls, pins, proves, the X control*) are EXAMPLES, not the test | a "device-signature control" claimed in prose and implemented by nothing |
CLAUDE.md:1710  >
CLAUDE.md:1711  > > *** THE VERB LIST WAS THE TEST UNTIL 2026-08-28, AND IT COULD NOT FIRE ON THE INCIDENT THAT
CLAUDE.md:1712  > > MADE THIS CHECK MANDATORY. Caught by the adjudicating seat while ruling on the mandate. ***
CLAUDE.md:1713  > >
CLAUDE.md:1714  > > `#489`'s load-bearing added comment reads *"An early return would skip that event and hang the
CLAUDE.md:1715  > > guest."* **It contains not one of the five verbs** -- it is a COUNTERFACTUAL-CONSEQUENCE claim
CLAUDE.md:1716  > > about a hypothetical edit, and the grep form walks straight past it. Mandating the block
CLAUDE.md:1717  > > unrepaired would have mandated a check that cannot fail on its own motivating case, which is
CLAUDE.md:1718  > > this file's check-that-cannot-fail shape arriving inside the repair for it.
CLAUDE.md:1719  > >
CLAUDE.md:1720  > > **So the trigger is SEMANTIC: does this sentence assert something a row could observe?** A
CLAUDE.md:1721  > > *"would &lt;consequence&gt;"* clause qualifies -- it names a channel (the event, the hang) and
CLAUDE.md:1722  > > predicts a difference -- and `#489` shipped exactly that channel's row only after a panel
CLAUDE.md:1723  > > found the escape three times.
CLAUDE.md:1724  > | **GUARD FIELD vs BODY FIELD** | list fields the PREDICATE reads and fields the guarded BODY reads; where they differ, grep whether they are written at the same sites. If not, the detector owes a state where they DISAGREE.  **Also record, per lvalue, the PARTITION THE BODY INDUCES on it** (its tested masks and compare regions) -- EXHAUSTED / SAMPLED is read off this column, never asserted (7n-5, 2026-10-09) | the guard's operand swapped for a field whose third state no row built |
CLAUDE.md:1725  > | **CHANNEL** | for each row, name **the mutant it kills** and **the observable that differs under that mutant**. A row whose named channel cannot vary under its named mutant is VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION, and that is knowable at writing time, before any table runs | a survival oracle at a REFUSAL-EFFECT site: guarded and unguarded code both survive |
CLAUDE.md:1726  >
CLAUDE.md:1727  > ### *** THE CHANNEL LINE IS THE FOURTH CHECK, ADDED 2026-08-28 AFTER `#489` SHIPPED A
CLAUDE.md:1728  > ### DETECTOR THREE TIMES AND A PANEL MEASURED FOUR ESCAPES OF ONE SHAPE. ***
CLAUDE.md:1729  >
CLAUDE.md:1730  > `#489` de-fatalised a guard, and **four separate mutants left the host ALIVE while the guard
CLAUDE.md:1731  > was wrong** -- `> 408`, exempt-zero, `< 408`, and a latch-state collapse. Each was caught
CLAUDE.md:1732  > only by a row reading a DECLINE channel (COND_DATA_AVAIL, the zeroed count, the fired event).
CLAUDE.md:1733  > Each was predicted by a seat from the packet alone, and **each escaped a table that had
CLAUDE.md:1734  > already come back CLEAN.** The round's own probe header carried the governing sentence before
CLAUDE.md:1735  > three of the four were found.
CLAUDE.md:1736  >
CLAUDE.md:1737  > > #### *** "EACH ESCAPED A CLEAN TABLE" IS FALSE OF `> 408`.  Corrected 2026-09-06 from the
CLAUDE.md:1738  > > #### retro re-check (batch 8b), verified against the artifacts before this edit. ***
CLAUDE.md:1739  > >
CLAUDE.md:1740  > > `regress/artifacts/gdromexitsix/killtable_20260828/M2_gt408.raw` -- TABLE 1, the 13-row instrument
CLAUDE.md:1741  > > -- reads `TOCLEN_RESULT=10/13`: the `> 408` mutant was KILLED from the first table and never escaped
CLAUDE.md:1742  > > anything (11/15 in table 2, 13/19 in table 4).  The clean-table escape of that shape was the EARLY
CLAUDE.md:1743  > > RETURN, `break;` -> `return;`: 13/13 on the 13-row table, then `M7_return.raw` 17/19 once `bad cnt`
CLAUDE.md:1744  > > and `bad event` were rowed.  Exempt-zero (`M8` 18/19), `< 408` (`M9` 18/19) and the latch collapse
CLAUDE.md:1745  > > (`M10` 18/19) stand as written.  **The rule above is untouched -- a row reading a DECLINE channel is
CLAUDE.md:1746  > > what caught every one of them -- and the exemplar's count is what was wrong.**  A wrong exemplar in
CLAUDE.md:1747  > > the paragraph that mints a check gets reused as the check's warrant, which is why it is corrected
CLAUDE.md:1748  > > here rather than left to the CHANGELOG (`#489`'s block carries the same correction).
CLAUDE.md:1749  >
CLAUDE.md:1750  > **THE COST WAS NOT MISSING KNOWLEDGE.  IT WAS KNOWLEDGE LIVING WHERE NOBODY CONSULTS IT AT
CLAUDE.md:1751  > ROW-WRITING TIME** -- this file's trigger-versus-memory finding, again.
CLAUDE.md:1752  >
CLAUDE.md:1753  > #### AND THE OBVIOUS GENERALISATION IS UNBOUNDED, SO IT IS NOT THE ONE ADOPTED
CLAUDE.md:1754  >
CLAUDE.md:1755  > The tempting sentence is *"every VALUE and every STATE in which a guard refuses needs a
CLAUDE.md:1756  > decline-channel row."*  **That licenses unbounded row growth -- the `#392` shape** -- and the
CLAUDE.md:1757  > adjudication that produced this line rejected it for exactly that reason. The distinction:
CLAUDE.md:1758  >
CLAUDE.md:1759  > * **ROW DEMAND** -- *which* refusals need pinning -- is ALREADY bounded and is not touched:
CLAUDE.md:1760  >   the floor clauses, the (d) DEPENDS-ON closure, one accept row per conditioned predicate,
CLAUDE.md:1761  >   one anchor row per position claim, one ask-again. **Rows are owed PER ENUMERATED MUTANT,
CLAUDE.md:1762  >   never per refusing state**; two states no enumerated mutant distinguishes collapse into one
CLAUDE.md:1763  >   row. That enumeration is diff-closed and finite.
CLAUDE.md:1764  > * **ROW VALIDITY** -- what `#489` actually measured -- is the CHANNEL line above, and it is a
CLAUDE.md:1765  >   WRITE-TIME check on rows that already exist.
CLAUDE.md:1766  >
CLAUDE.md:1767  > > **A FILTER CAN REJECT OR REPAIR A ROW; IT CAN NEVER DEMAND A NEW ONE -- SO IT CANNOT GROW
CLAUDE.md:1768  > > WITHOUT BOUND.**  That is the stop condition, and it is structural rather than a cap: the
CLAUDE.md:1769  > > generator side is untouched.
CLAUDE.md:1770  >
CLAUDE.md:1771  > *Mechanising it -- a `# kills: <mutant> via <channel>` comment per row -- is FILED, not
CLAUDE.md:1772  > adopted: it changes what every probe in the tree must carry, and that is an owner decision
CLAUDE.md:1773  > rather than a round's.*
CLAUDE.md:1774  >
CLAUDE.md:1775  > **The third is the one that looked impossible and is the cheapest.** It only seemed
CLAUDE.md:1776  > unreachable because it was approached from the mutant side -- *"what edit escapes?"*,
CLAUDE.md:1777  > which is unbounded -- instead of the field side -- *"what states can these two fields be
CLAUDE.md:1778  > in?"*, which is a grep. In `#462` that was three lines of output: `alloc_data` sets both
CLAUDE.md:1779  > fields; two `free` sites clear only one.
CLAUDE.md:1780  >
CLAUDE.md:1781  > **DO NOT move the ask-again earlier.** It works BECAUSE a clean table redirects seats away
CLAUDE.md:1782  > from shapes the author already covered; measured yield says so. And do not raise the cap
CLAUDE.md:1783  > -- the cap is what stands between this and `#392`.
CLAUDE.md:1784  
CLAUDE.md:1785  > **(e) THE CONTRACT.** The probe header names which edits this detector is designed
CLAUDE.md:1786  > to catch. *A detector that says so is falsifiable; one claiming completeness is
CLAUDE.md:1787  > not* -- and a later escape becomes "outside the contract" or "a breach of it",
CLAUDE.md:1788  > which are different dispositions, rather than an argument about overclaiming.
CLAUDE.md:1789  > Two lines in a header.
CLAUDE.md:1790  >
CLAUDE.md:1791  > **IT IS A FLOOR CLAUSE, so a breach is L2 �?" replace IN-ROUND however late it is found.**
CLAUDE.md:1792  > That is deliberate and it is the whole point: an escaped (d) mutant means the round's
CLAUDE.md:1793  > claim to have "shipped a detector with the fix" is false about the fix's own repair.
CLAUDE.md:1794  >

======== CLAUDE.md:2373-2386 (coverage debt above the floor) ========
CLAUDE.md:2373  > **Above floor, measured escapes are COVERAGE DEBT: FILE them**, with the escape list recorded,
CLAUDE.md:2374  > ranked by what they would admit �?" and ranked BELOW live defects in the same file.
CLAUDE.md:2375  
CLAUDE.md:2376  The floor is not invented: it is what four earlier rounds already did. `sh4bcr`'s first detector
CLAUDE.md:2377  passed a mutant reinstating the very host kill its round removed; `sh4pcicexit`'s passed the
CLAUDE.md:2378  original kill verbatim; `pitflip`'s rewind had zero coverage; #446's passed a **217-byte
CLAUDE.md:2379  comment-only file**. All four strengthened or replaced IN-ROUND, and all four were below this
CLAUDE.md:2380  floor. #447's detector is above it, checked row by row �?" so filing its nine escapes was right.
CLAUDE.md:2381  
CLAUDE.md:2382  **Reading "MEASURED FALSE PASS" as "any measured mutant escape" would reproduce #392**: the
CLAUDE.md:2383  measuring seat finds escapes essentially every round and the mutation space is unbounded. The
CLAUDE.md:2384  severity rule settles the ordering �?" #447's escapes are *hypothetical edits in uncovered product
CLAUDE.md:2385  cells*, while `sh4chcr` is a **measured live host death in the shipped tree**.
CLAUDE.md:2386  

======== CLAUDE.md:2448-2484 (the ask-again and its cap) ========
CLAUDE.md:2448  > ### *** THE ASK-AGAIN, ADOPTED 2026-08-22, AND ITS CAP IS THE WHOLE SAFETY. ***
CLAUDE.md:2449  >
CLAUDE.md:2450  > **Once a detector's kill table is CLEAN, ask the panel once more for another mutant.**
CLAUDE.md:2451  > Measured yield: 2 of the 5 rows #451 added, and 6 of the 15 in #452, came only from that
CLAUDE.md:2452  > second ask �?" after the first table was already green. The mechanism is real: a clean table
CLAUDE.md:2453  > redirects seats away from the shapes the author already thought of.
CLAUDE.md:2454  >
CLAUDE.md:2455  > **CAPPED AT ONE ASK-AGAIN PER DETECTOR PER ROUND.** This is not the #392 chain �?" #392 was
CLAUDE.md:2456  > unbounded fix-of-fix passes with no stop condition; this is a single extra adversarial query
CLAUDE.md:2457  > against a clean table with a measured stop: nothing found, done; something found, row added
CLAUDE.md:2458  > under L1, done. **The cap IS the anti-#392 clause.** A second ask-again after an empty first
CLAUDE.md:2459  > is exactly where the chain starts, and anything found past the cap is FILED, never fixed.
CLAUDE.md:2460  >
CLAUDE.md:2461  > > ### *** THE CAP COUNTS **ASKS**, NEVER **FINDINGS**. Adjudicated 2026-08-28, because the
CLAUDE.md:2462  > > ### singular in the sentence above ("row added", "something found") invited the opposite
CLAUDE.md:2463  > > ### reading and that reading NEARLY FILED A FLOOR BREACH. ***
CLAUDE.md:2464  > >
CLAUDE.md:2465  > > Everything ONE ask returns is disposed of INDIVIDUALLY under L1�?"L4 and the floor �?" and **a
CLAUDE.md:2466  > > floor breach among the findings is L2, repaired in-round however late it is found.** A
CLAUDE.md:2467  > > second ASK is where the #392 chain starts; the number of rows one ask yields is not.
CLAUDE.md:2468  > >
CLAUDE.md:2469  > > **The founding measurement already said so and the wording contradicted it:** this block's
CLAUDE.md:2470  > > own text records *"6 of the 15 in #452 came only from that second ask."* A reading under
CLAUDE.md:2471  > > which #452 should have taken one row and filed five is refuted by the paragraph that mints
CLAUDE.md:2472  > > the rule.
CLAUDE.md:2473  > >
CLAUDE.md:2474  > > **The live cost of the misreading, from `#488`:** three escapes were found by one ask; the
CLAUDE.md:2475  > > author took one and filed two, reasoning "the cap says I may take only one". `E1` was
CLAUDE.md:2476  > > `if (X)` -> `if (X && Y)` on the DOMINATING predicate �?" the canonical clause-(d) form, i.e.
CLAUDE.md:2477  > > a floor breach, which the floor says must be repaired in-round. **So one rule's cap was
CLAUDE.md:2478  > > about to silently override another rule's MUST.** A reading that produces that collision is
CLAUDE.md:2479  > > wrong on its face.
CLAUDE.md:2480  > >
CLAUDE.md:2481  > > The anti-runaway property is untouched: the bounded resource was always the adversarial
CLAUDE.md:2482  > > QUERY, and what it returns is bounded by being the findings of ONE query against ONE clean
CLAUDE.md:2483  > > table.
CLAUDE.md:2484  

======== CLAUDE.md:2590-2704 (the four in-round licences (L1-L4), the stopping rule, THE REPAIR CHAIN and its class-closure amendment) ========
CLAUDE.md:2590  > ### THE FOUR REASONS THAT LICENSE AN IN-ROUND FIX OF DETECTOR MATERIAL
CLAUDE.md:2591  >
CLAUDE.md:2592  > Everything else is FILED. Named so no round has to re-argue it:
CLAUDE.md:2593  >
CLAUDE.md:2594  > * **L1 �?" pre-ship construction.** The row is part of the deliverable still being built. A row
CLAUDE.md:2595  >   measured vacuous before commit is unfinished, not a reopening. **The stopping rule governs
CLAUDE.md:2596  >   findings against SHIPPED material only.**
CLAUDE.md:2597  > * **L2 �?" floor breach.** The shipped detector fails vacuity-floor clause (a), (b) or (c) �?"
CLAUDE.md:2598  >   replace in-round however late it is found.
CLAUDE.md:2599  > * **L3 �?" undischarged obligation.** The row is the sole carrier of something the round OWES
CLAUDE.md:2600  >   (a de-escalation accept row; the "shipped a detector" claim itself). A vacuous sole carrier
CLAUDE.md:2601  >   makes the round's record false.
CLAUDE.md:2602  > * **L4 �?" wrong record.** Prose or comments claiming coverage a measurement refutes.
CLAUDE.md:2603  
CLAUDE.md:2604  This resolves an apparent inconsistency: #447's escapes were FILED while #448's and #449's were
CLAUDE.md:2605  fixed in-round. #447's were measured against a **shipped** detector sitting above the floor �?"
CLAUDE.md:2606  coverage debt. #448's and #449's were caught **inside their own construction** (L1), and #448's
CLAUDE.md:2607  `return`�+'`break` variant additionally re-reached the removed `exit(1)`, which is L2.
CLAUDE.md:2608  
CLAUDE.md:2609  **A disjointness assertion over the latch classes was REJECTED, on three measured grounds** �?" the
CLAUDE.md:2610  class bit-domains overlap pervasively today, so the general form is unsatisfiable; separation
CLAUDE.md:2611  rests on the array INDICES rather than on the constants, so the assert would guard the wrong
CLAUDE.md:2612  invariant; and for the one disjoint pair the values are hardware register layouts that cannot
CLAUDE.md:2613  legitimately change, so it could never fire. **The real hazard is a duplicate class ID**, and its
CLAUDE.md:2614  zero-cost structural fix is converting the `SH4_VAL_*` defines to a C99 enum ending in
CLAUDE.md:2615  `_NCLASS`, where duplicates are impossible by construction.
CLAUDE.md:2616  
CLAUDE.md:2617  *** DISCHARGED -- DO NOT REDO IT. Verified 2026-08-23 by opening the file. *** The trigger
CLAUDE.md:2618  was "when a tenth class arrives"; `SH4_VAL_SCICMD` was the tenth, the conversion happened,
CLAUDE.md:2619  and `dev_sh4.c:124` is now `enum {` ending in `SH4_VAL_NCLASS`, with the file's own comment
CLAUDE.md:2620  at `:119-122` recording that the pre-set trigger fired. An eleventh class is therefore free.
CLAUDE.md:2621  This paragraph is kept for its reasoning; its instruction is complete.
CLAUDE.md:2622  
CLAUDE.md:2623  This applies to the whole #438 / #441 / #443 / #447 family, and #447's own probe already *stated*
CLAUDE.md:2624  the hazard in its constants block ("a mask pinned only from BELOW �?� cannot see a guard that
CLAUDE.md:2625  GREW") while shipping no row for it. **The file knew. The rows just did not exist.**
CLAUDE.md:2626  
CLAUDE.md:2627  **THE STOPPING RULE, and it is the one that was missing.** #392 ran SIX follow-up passes,
CLAUDE.md:2628  each finding defects in the previous pass's fixes, and the chain did not converge.
CLAUDE.md:2629  > A review finding gets FIXED IN THIS ROUND only if it is a MEASURED FALSE PASS or a WRONG
CLAUDE.md:2630  > RECORD. Everything else gets FILED.
CLAUDE.md:2631  That rule alone would have ended #392 at pass-2c instead of 2f.
CLAUDE.md:2632  
CLAUDE.md:2633  > ### *** THE REPAIR CHAIN -- a repair made in response to a pass 2 OWES ITS OWN PASS 2.
CLAUDE.md:2634  > ### Adjudicated 2026-08-31, then CORRECTED TWICE by the adjudicating seat in the same day,
CLAUDE.md:2635  > ### and both corrections came from the chain that minted it. ***
CLAUDE.md:2636  >
CLAUDE.md:2637  > **THE OBLIGATION FOLLOWS THE MATERIAL, NOT THE NARRATIVE.** A round that repairs a detector
CLAUDE.md:2638  > in response to a panel has produced NEW floor-bound material, and "the round already had its
CLAUDE.md:2639  > pass 2" is a fact about the narrative. `#499` ran four passes and **the first three each
CLAUDE.md:2640  > found a real defect, two of them MEASURED CLEAN SWEEPS of a detector that had just come back
CLAUDE.md:2641  > green.**
CLAUDE.md:2642  >
CLAUDE.md:2643  > **THE STOP CONDITION: one pass per floor-bound diff, closing on the FIRST PASS THAT FINDS
CLAUDE.md:2644  > ZERO BREACHES** -- subject to the two corrections below, without which it closes early on a
CLAUDE.md:2645  > live escape.
CLAUDE.md:2646  >
CLAUDE.md:2647  > #### CORRECTION 1 -- A ZERO NEEDS A VALIDITY CONDITION ON THE PASS, AND A DENOMINATOR
CLAUDE.md:2648  >
CLAUDE.md:2649  > As first written, "zero breaches" counted a pass's OUTPUT with no condition on the pass
CLAUDE.md:2650  > itself. **Three briefs that day went out with the diff missing** -- a `git show | head -N`
CLAUDE.md:2651  > inlined the commit message instead -- and a panel answering "no breaches" against an empty
CLAUDE.md:2652  > packet would have CLOSED THE CHAIN.
CLAUDE.md:2653  >
CLAUDE.md:2654  > > **A closing pass must be a VALID pass: the diff actually delivered; the roster complete or
CLAUDE.md:2655  > > its misses named per the seat ledger; and the zero carried WITH ITS DENOMINATOR -- which
CLAUDE.md:2656  > > cells, which mutant shapes, were swept.** *"Zero found"* and *"zero looked for"* must not
CLAUDE.md:2657  > > render the same, which is this project's oldest rule about blanks turned on its own stop
CLAUDE.md:2658  > > condition.
CLAUDE.md:2659  >
CLAUDE.md:2660  > That also converts the rule's finishing incentive into a checkable artifact: **a bare zero
CLAUDE.md:2661  > does not close; a zero with an enumeration does.**
CLAUDE.md:2662  >
CLAUDE.md:2663  > #### CORRECTION 2 -- SCOPE: EFFORT ON THE DIFF, THE FLOOR QUESTION ON THE COMPOSITE
CLAUDE.md:2664  >
CLAUDE.md:2665  > *"Scoped to that diff"* is ambiguous and **the dangerous reading would have closed `#499`
CLAUDE.md:2666  > over a live total escape.** The breach that pass 3 found was not in that pass's two hunks --
CLAUDE.md:2667  > the hunks were fine. It lived in the SHIPPED COMPOSITE detector's coverage of the ORIGINAL
CLAUDE.md:2668  > fix's mutant space.
CLAUDE.md:2669  >
CLAUDE.md:2670  > > **Review EFFORT is scoped to the repair diff, so settled design is not re-litigated. The
CLAUDE.md:2671  > > FLOOR QUESTION -- does a clean-sweep mutant exist in the (d) enumeration -- is asked of the
CLAUDE.md:2672  > > SHIPPED COMPOSITE on every pass.**
CLAUDE.md:2673  >
CLAUDE.md:2674  > #### THE BOUND IS IMPORTED, NOT INTRINSIC -- state the dependency or the rule overclaims
CLAUDE.md:2675  >
CLAUDE.md:2676  > The stop condition does not bound the chain and must not pretend to. The bound comes from
CLAUDE.md:2677  > **the (d) DEPENDS-ON enumeration being diff-closed and finite**, and from *zero breaches*
CLAUDE.md:2678  > meaning **zero SURVIVING ADJUDICATION** rather than zero raised -- otherwise one stubborn
CLAUDE.md:2679  > seat blocks closure forever, against the count-arguments-not-seats rule. With those imports
CLAUDE.md:2680  > the chain is infinite only if every repair is defective, in which case every pass is buying a
CLAUDE.md:2681  > real defect off the ship.
CLAUDE.md:2682  >
CLAUDE.md:2683  > #### *** THE AMENDMENT THAT ACTUALLY ENDS CHAINS, and it is the part worth carrying. ***
CLAUDE.md:2684  >
CLAUDE.md:2685  > `#499`'s three breaches were **THE SAME CLASS THREE TIMES** -- a cell of one state space left
CLAUDE.md:2686  > empty, repaired at exactly the address the mutant had used, leaving the symmetric cell open
CLAUDE.md:2687  > for the next pass. Pass 1 found *width* unpinned; pass 2 found *component*; pass 3 found
CLAUDE.md:2688  > *direction* AND *position*. **Pass-per-instance converges LINEARLY IN CELLS**, and 28 -> 34
CLAUDE.md:2689  > -> 36 rows bought one cell each.
CLAUDE.md:2690  >
CLAUDE.md:2691  > > **WHEN TWO CONSECUTIVE PASSES FIND BREACHES OF ONE CLASS, THE NEXT REPAIR OWES THE CLASS
CLAUDE.md:2692  > > CLOSURE: enumerate the full space from the function's own inputs, close every cell in ONE
CLAUDE.md:2693  > > diff, and write into the CONTRACT which cells are covered and which are deliberately not.
CLAUDE.md:2694  > > The closing pass then VERIFIES THE ENUMERATION rather than hunting cells.**
CLAUDE.md:2695  >
CLAUDE.md:2696  > That is the reading-seat-buys-the-class lesson wearing the repair hat. It also converts a
CLAUDE.md:2697  > future escape from *"another breach"* into *"outside the contract, as stated"* -- which is
CLAUDE.md:2698  > exactly what clause (e) is for, and which a cell-by-cell chain can never reach.
CLAUDE.md:2699  >
CLAUDE.md:2700  > *Provenance: proposed by the main loop, adjudicated by the flagship seat AGAINST ITSELF --
CLAUDE.md:2701  > it was asked to be harder on a rule that favours finishing, and returned two defects and an
CLAUDE.md:2702  > amendment. The measuring seat independently reached the same amendment from the other end:
CLAUDE.md:2703  > "adding two more rows at the corner I just named will produce a fourth pass."*
CLAUDE.md:2704  


