1598: > #### *** A DERIVED INPUT IS GRADED BY WHAT THE BODY TESTS ON IT, NEVER BY THE FLOW THAT REACHES IT.
1599: > #### Ruled 2026-10-09 (Fable 5.1, queue 7n-5; GPT-6 Astra confirmed with corrections), written by
1600: > #### owner ruling. ***
1601: >
1602: > A local computed from the function's inputs and feeding a body-read operand gets the partition the
1603: > body induces on IT: `#519`'s `tile_nr` -- tested `>= 256` on the tiled branch and `>= 32` on the
1604: > LIN_A branch, never for zero -- is clause (e), not floor, and so is its tile-index escape family.
1605: > Data flow alone is the transitive chasing the DEPENDS-ON bound forbids; a dispatch LABEL is a floor
1606: > axis only by the label clauses above.  **"Closable by a finite row set" is NOT the discriminator**: it
1607: > is the 09-04 finiteness bound under a new name, and every single slice is one-row closable while the
1608: > family never is -- so it separated nothing, and one species was graded two ways by it.
1609: >
1610: > **EXHAUSTED / SAMPLED is adopted as clause-(e) disclosure vocabulary**: EXHAUSTED = every cell of the
1611: > BODY-TESTED partition of that lvalue is rowed (never "every value in the domain"); SAMPLED = driven at
1612: > two or more values, with the untested slices and their derived or measured sweeps named.  A
1613: > disclosure vocabulary can never demand a row.  Its landing spot is the PRE-TABLE CHECK's GUARD FIELD
1614: > vs BODY FIELD row below, which now also records the partition the body induces on each lvalue.
1615: 
1616: > ### *** THE PRE-TABLE CHECK -- three greps, ~30 min, and it protects the ASK-AGAIN'S
1617: > ### CAP rather than the clock. Adopted 2026-08-24; two seats derived it independently. ***
1618: >
1619: > > ## *** MANDATORY as of 2026-08-28, by flagship adjudication. It was RECOMMENDED prose for
1620: > > ## four days and TWO rounds skipped it -- which is this file's own structure-first trigger,
1621: > > ## met exactly. ***
1622: > >
1623: > > **SCOPE, and this is what answers the cost objection: it binds EXACTLY the population the
1624: > > vacuity floor already binds** -- any round shipping or EXTENDING a kill-table-backed
1625: > > detector. **Not every round.** Records-only, documented-only and table-less harness rounds
1626: > > are untouched.
1627: > >
1628: > > **FORM: recorded OUTPUT, never assertion** -- the two-part-clearance precedent, *run it, do
1629: > > not assert it*. One `PRETABLE.out` in the round's kill-table artifacts dir (or a probe-header
1630: > > section), **written BEFORE table 1 runs so its own mtime proves the order** -- the same
1631: > > order discriminator the oracle-correction rule uses. It carries: the sibling arms compared
1632: > > and any asymmetry; each enumerated claim sentence mapped to a row or an owed sentence; the
1633: > > guard-field/body-field lists and any disagreeing-state row owed; and per row, the mutant it
1634: > > kills and the observable that differs.
1635: > >
1636: > > **Any check may discharge `N/A` WITH ITS REASON RECORDED. An unrun check and an inapplicable
1637: > > one must not render the same** -- this project's oldest rule about blanks.
1638: > >
1639: > > ### *** AND A THIRD FAILURE MODE, MEASURED 2026-08-28: THE BLOCK WAS RUN, ITS ROWS WERE
1640: > > ### PRESCRIBED, AND THEY WERE LOST WHEN THE ROW LIST WAS REWRITTEN THREE HOURS LATER. ***
1641: > >
1642: > > `#490`'s `PRETABLE.out` named two mutants at **16:05** -- a latch-bit collision and a brace
1643: > > slip -- and prescribed a row for each (`crossbit`, `latch cond`). At **18:45** a nine-seat
1644: > > panel found a DIFFERENT defect (the row set was site-asymmetric), the addition list was
1645: > > rewritten to carry the panel's findings, and **both earlier rows silently vanished.** The
1646: > > measuring seat then BUILT both mutants and both escaped the committed 13-row detector --
1647: > > two clause-(d) floor breaches that the round's own artifact had predicted and specified.
1648: > >
1649: > > **Nothing was forgotten in the sense of never known.** The knowledge sat in the file that
1650: > > exists precisely to be consulted at row-writing time, and did not survive a rewrite in a
1651: > > DIFFERENT artifact. The new list was not careless -- it correctly added everything the
1652: > > panel had just found.
1653: > >
1654: > > > **A ROW LIST REWRITTEN FROM A PANEL'S FINDINGS IS DIFFED AGAINST THE PRE-TABLE BLOCK'S
1655: > > > OWN PRESCRIBED ROWS, NOT MERELY EXTENDED WITH THE NEW ONES.** The block PRESCRIBES rows;
1656: > > > a later list that does not contain them has dropped an obligation, and the diff is the
1657: > > > only thing that says so.
1658: > >
1659: > > *This is the block's own lesson turned on the block: it exists because a recommended check
1660: > > is memory and memory does not fire. Running it once does not make its OUTPUT durable --
1661: > > the output has to be carried forward, and nothing was checking that it was.*
1662: > >
1663: > > **AND THE GUARD-FIELD CHECK COULD NOT HAVE CAUGHT EITHER**, which is worth knowing before
1664: > > relying on it: it asks where the guard field and the body field DIFFER. For the latch
1665: > > collision they are the SAME field (`d->warned`). **For a shared bitfield the question is
1666: > > *"who else writes this field, and can they set my bit?"*** -- measured here as
1667: > > `grep -c 'd->warned |='` = 8 sites, **6 of them inside `DEVICE_ACCESS`**, so most bits are
1668: > > set by ordinary guest register reads and a collision would be silenced by benign traffic.
1669: >
1670: > > **THE TRIGGER WAS MET TWICE IN FOUR DAYS, and the value at each was specific rather than
1671: > > general:** at `#485` the GUARD-FIELD grep enumerates `D1..D5` mechanically, because the
1672: > > `default:` arm's `fatal()` arguments ARE the guarded body's field reads; at `#489` the
1673: > > CHANNEL line names the one shape all four escapes shared -- **and the probe header carried
1674: > > the governing sentence before three of the four were found.** Knowledge present, unconsulted
1675: > > at row-writing time. *A recommended check is memory, and this file's finding is that memory
1676: > > does not fire.*
1677: > >
1678: > > **ENFORCEMENT is existence-only and FILED, not built:** a commit adding a killtable artifacts
1679: > > dir must add `PRETABLE.out` beside it, forward-only from a cutoff. That is a tooling change
1680: > > owing two negative controls, so it ships as its own harness round rather than as a rider.
1681: > > Until it lands the mandate lives HERE, in lifecycle steps 4-5, and in `/round`.
1682: > >
1683: > > *** AND THE FIRST MANDATED APPLICATION MUST **CHECK** THE CHANNEL LINE, NOT MERELY OBEY IT.
1684: > > *** CHANNEL is one day old, and this file's freshly-minted-exemplar rule says a new rule's
1685: > > example is a HYPOTHESIS. Its claim is that a row's channel-under-its-mutant is knowable at
1686: > > WRITING time, before any table runs. One sentence in the first `PRETABLE.out` says whether
1687: > > that held.
1688: > >
1689: > > *Provenance note, because the ruling corrected my own citation: the twice-skipped rounds are
1690: > > `#485` (wdcnoirq) and `#489`, **not `#486`** -- `#486` is wdcfloodstale's non-discriminating
1691: > > gate row. The substance was intact and the number was wrong, and a wrong number in a
1692: > > mandate's provenance gets reused.*
1693: >
1694: > *** THE DIAGNOSIS THAT PROMPTED IT WAS WRONG, AND THE CORRECTION IS THE USEFUL PART. ***
1695: > `#462` ran a 12-mutant table clean, then one capped ask-again found escapes needing five
1696: > rows -- and adding rows changed other mutants' scores, forcing a **second four-hour
1697: > table**. The adjudicator blamed obligations caught too late. **A seat then checked the
1698: > preserved logs and refuted that: both "late" rows were in the FIRST table already.** They
1699: > cost nothing. What cost four hours was that **the probe cannot score one row** -- 39
1700: > `session()` calls, no row filter -- so re-measuring five rows meant re-running everything.
1701: >
1702: > **So the checklist does NOT save the table.** Its real value is narrower and it is worth
1703: > having: **the ask-again is capped at ONE, and every hole it spends itself naming is a
1704: > query not spent on coverage debt.** A detector arriving with visible holes wastes the cap.
1705: >
1706: > | check | how | catches |
1707: > |---|---|---|
1708: > | **SIBLING PARITY** | for each arm the diff de-fatalises, compare its observable channels against its nearest sibling arm. An asymmetry is a row you owe or a sentence you owe | a de-fatalised arm with NO completion-event row while its sibling had one |
1709: > | **CLAIM -> ROW** | every ADDED comment sentence asserting an OBSERVABLE CONSEQUENCE owes a row or an explicit no-row sentence. Claim verbs (*observes, controls, pins, proves, the X control*) are EXAMPLES, not the test | a "device-signature control" claimed in prose and implemented by nothing |
1710: >
1711: > > *** THE VERB LIST WAS THE TEST UNTIL 2026-08-28, AND IT COULD NOT FIRE ON THE INCIDENT THAT
1712: > > MADE THIS CHECK MANDATORY. Caught by the adjudicating seat while ruling on the mandate. ***
1713: > >
1714: > > `#489`'s load-bearing added comment reads *"An early return would skip that event and hang the
1715: > > guest."* **It contains not one of the five verbs** -- it is a COUNTERFACTUAL-CONSEQUENCE claim
1716: > > about a hypothetical edit, and the grep form walks straight past it. Mandating the block
1717: > > unrepaired would have mandated a check that cannot fail on its own motivating case, which is
1718: > > this file's check-that-cannot-fail shape arriving inside the repair for it.
1719: > >
1720: > > **So the trigger is SEMANTIC: does this sentence assert something a row could observe?** A
1721: > > *"would &lt;consequence&gt;"* clause qualifies -- it names a channel (the event, the hang) and
1722: > > predicts a difference -- and `#489` shipped exactly that channel's row only after a panel
1723: > > found the escape three times.
1724: > | **GUARD FIELD vs BODY FIELD** | list fields the PREDICATE reads and fields the guarded BODY reads; where they differ, grep whether they are written at the same sites. If not, the detector owes a state where they DISAGREE.  **Also record, per lvalue, the PARTITION THE BODY INDUCES on it** (its tested masks and compare regions) -- EXHAUSTED / SAMPLED is read off this column, never asserted (7n-5, 2026-10-09) | the guard's operand swapped for a field whose third state no row built |
1725: > | **CHANNEL** | for each row, name **the mutant it kills** and **the observable that differs under that mutant**. A row whose named channel cannot vary under its named mutant is VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION, and that is knowable at writing time, before any table runs | a survival oracle at a REFUSAL-EFFECT site: guarded and unguarded code both survive |
1726: >
1727: > ### *** THE CHANNEL LINE IS THE FOURTH CHECK, ADDED 2026-08-28 AFTER `#489` SHIPPED A
1728: > ### DETECTOR THREE TIMES AND A PANEL MEASURED FOUR ESCAPES OF ONE SHAPE. ***
1729: >
1730: > `#489` de-fatalised a guard, and **four separate mutants left the host ALIVE while the guard
1731: > was wrong** -- `> 408`, exempt-zero, `< 408`, and a latch-state collapse. Each was caught
1732: > only by a row reading a DECLINE channel (COND_DATA_AVAIL, the zeroed count, the fired event).
1733: > Each was predicted by a seat from the packet alone, and **each escaped a table that had
1734: > already come back CLEAN.** The round's own probe header carried the governing sentence before
