2624: the hazard in its constants block ("a mask pinned only from BELOW �?� cannot see a guard that
2625: GREW") while shipping no row for it. **The file knew. The rows just did not exist.**
2626: 
2627: **THE STOPPING RULE, and it is the one that was missing.** #392 ran SIX follow-up passes,
2628: each finding defects in the previous pass's fixes, and the chain did not converge.
2629: > A review finding gets FIXED IN THIS ROUND only if it is a MEASURED FALSE PASS or a WRONG
2630: > RECORD. Everything else gets FILED.
2631: That rule alone would have ended #392 at pass-2c instead of 2f.
2632: 
2633: > ### *** THE REPAIR CHAIN -- a repair made in response to a pass 2 OWES ITS OWN PASS 2.
2634: > ### Adjudicated 2026-08-31, then CORRECTED TWICE by the adjudicating seat in the same day,
2635: > ### and both corrections came from the chain that minted it. ***
2636: >
2637: > **THE OBLIGATION FOLLOWS THE MATERIAL, NOT THE NARRATIVE.** A round that repairs a detector
2638: > in response to a panel has produced NEW floor-bound material, and "the round already had its
2639: > pass 2" is a fact about the narrative. `#499` ran four passes and **the first three each
2640: > found a real defect, two of them MEASURED CLEAN SWEEPS of a detector that had just come back
2641: > green.**
2642: >
2643: > **THE STOP CONDITION: one pass per floor-bound diff, closing on the FIRST PASS THAT FINDS
2644: > ZERO BREACHES** -- subject to the two corrections below, without which it closes early on a
2645: > live escape.
2646: >
2647: > #### CORRECTION 1 -- A ZERO NEEDS A VALIDITY CONDITION ON THE PASS, AND A DENOMINATOR
2648: >
2649: > As first written, "zero breaches" counted a pass's OUTPUT with no condition on the pass
2650: > itself. **Three briefs that day went out with the diff missing** -- a `git show | head -N`
2651: > inlined the commit message instead -- and a panel answering "no breaches" against an empty
2652: > packet would have CLOSED THE CHAIN.
2653: >
2654: > > **A closing pass must be a VALID pass: the diff actually delivered; the roster complete or
2655: > > its misses named per the seat ledger; and the zero carried WITH ITS DENOMINATOR -- which
2656: > > cells, which mutant shapes, were swept.** *"Zero found"* and *"zero looked for"* must not
2657: > > render the same, which is this project's oldest rule about blanks turned on its own stop
2658: > > condition.
2659: >
2660: > That also converts the rule's finishing incentive into a checkable artifact: **a bare zero
2661: > does not close; a zero with an enumeration does.**
2662: >
2663: > #### CORRECTION 2 -- SCOPE: EFFORT ON THE DIFF, THE FLOOR QUESTION ON THE COMPOSITE
2664: >
2665: > *"Scoped to that diff"* is ambiguous and **the dangerous reading would have closed `#499`
2666: > over a live total escape.** The breach that pass 3 found was not in that pass's two hunks --
2667: > the hunks were fine. It lived in the SHIPPED COMPOSITE detector's coverage of the ORIGINAL
2668: > fix's mutant space.
2669: >
2670: > > **Review EFFORT is scoped to the repair diff, so settled design is not re-litigated. The
2671: > > FLOOR QUESTION -- does a clean-sweep mutant exist in the (d) enumeration -- is asked of the
2672: > > SHIPPED COMPOSITE on every pass.**
2673: >
2674: > #### THE BOUND IS IMPORTED, NOT INTRINSIC -- state the dependency or the rule overclaims
2675: >
2676: > The stop condition does not bound the chain and must not pretend to. The bound comes from
2677: > **the (d) DEPENDS-ON enumeration being diff-closed and finite**, and from *zero breaches*
2678: > meaning **zero SURVIVING ADJUDICATION** rather than zero raised -- otherwise one stubborn
2679: > seat blocks closure forever, against the count-arguments-not-seats rule. With those imports
2680: > the chain is infinite only if every repair is defective, in which case every pass is buying a
2681: > real defect off the ship.
2682: >
2683: > #### *** THE AMENDMENT THAT ACTUALLY ENDS CHAINS, and it is the part worth carrying. ***
2684: >
2685: > `#499`'s three breaches were **THE SAME CLASS THREE TIMES** -- a cell of one state space left
2686: > empty, repaired at exactly the address the mutant had used, leaving the symmetric cell open
2687: > for the next pass. Pass 1 found *width* unpinned; pass 2 found *component*; pass 3 found
2688: > *direction* AND *position*. **Pass-per-instance converges LINEARLY IN CELLS**, and 28 -> 34
2689: > -> 36 rows bought one cell each.
2690: >
2691: > > **WHEN TWO CONSECUTIVE PASSES FIND BREACHES OF ONE CLASS, THE NEXT REPAIR OWES THE CLASS
2692: > > CLOSURE: enumerate the full space from the function's own inputs, close every cell in ONE
2693: > > diff, and write into the CONTRACT which cells are covered and which are deliberately not.
2694: > > The closing pass then VERIFIES THE ENUMERATION rather than hunting cells.**
2695: >
2696: > That is the reading-seat-buys-the-class lesson wearing the repair hat. It also converts a
2697: > future escape from *"another breach"* into *"outside the contract, as stated"* -- which is
2698: > exactly what clause (e) is for, and which a cell-by-cell chain can never reach.
2699: >
2700: > *Provenance: proposed by the main loop, adjudicated by the flagship seat AGAINST ITSELF --
2701: > it was asked to be harder on a rule that favours finishing, and returned two defects and an
2702: > amendment. The measuring seat independently reached the same amendment from the other end:
2703: > "adding two more rows at the corner I just named will produce a fourth pass."*
