118: ## 3. The rows
119: 
120: Both are new rows. `row_pio_sector_irq` (`REPAIR3.c.txt:7049-7077`) stays byte-identical. Fable's one-line edit of that row (allocate `atapi_st`, keep `a255 == 0 && a256 == 1`) does kill `D2_st` and `D2_or_st`, because with length 0 the mutant asserts on the first read and `a255` becomes nonzero while HEAD stays green (`fable5_r3pass.md:34`; Argon agrees, `gemini_web.out:33`). It leaves (flag 0, `atapi_len` > 0) open, which is the cell the previous pass's one-cell repair left open on the other guard. The class closure owes both cells (`tools/CLAUDE.md:2691-2694`).
121: 
122: **Row 1. `PIO: stale xfer, no early irq`** (29 characters).
123: 
124: Fixture. `rep_fresh` (`REPAIR3.c.txt:6452-6463`): flag 0, phase 0, `atapi_len` 0, `atapi_received` 0, `atapi_st` NULL, `int_assert` 0, sink `rep_sink`. Then synthesise the post-dispatch state the writers produce: `atapi_st = scsi_transfer_alloc()` (`REPAIR3.c.txt:357-359`); `data_in` one malloc'd byte, non-NULL; `data_in_len = 36`; `atapi_received = 36`; `atapi_len` left 0; flag and phase left 0. Queue 2 bytes with `wdc_addtoinbuf`. `rep_asserts = 0`. One `dev_wdc_access` of `wd_data`, width 2, `MEM_READ`.
125: 
126: Oracle: `rep_asserts == 0 && warned_null_xfer == 0 && rep_occ == 0`.
127: 
128: HEAD. Flag 0, so the PIO arm (`dev_wdc.c:1468`). Two bytes pop. The ring is empty, so `inbuf_tail != inbuf_head` is false and `dev_wdc.c:1472` does not set `int_assert`. Latch stays 0. Occupancy 0. Green.
129: 
130: Kills `D2_st` and `D2_or_st`. The mutant enters `dev_wdc.c:1228`. Width 2 wants consumed 2; the clamp at `dev_wdc.c:1290-1291` stores 0 because `atapi_len` is 0. Length stays 0, received stays 36. `dev_wdc.c:1312` is false (`atapi_st` non-NULL). `dev_wdc.c:1318-1319` is true (length 0, phase 0, and `PHASE_CMDOUT` is `0x09`). `dev_wdc.c:1359` is false (36 < 36). The else at `dev_wdc.c:1464-1466` sets `int_assert`. The tick at `dev_wdc.c:2048` makes `rep_asserts == 1`. Red.
131: 
132: CHANNEL: INTRQ (`rep_asserts` 1 versus 0). Occupancy is 0 on both, because the pops run at `dev_wdc.c:1198-1213` before the flag. The latch is 0 on both. Ireason and CYL do not move, because both are gated on the flag (`dev_wdc.c:1946`, `:1973`) and the flag stays 0.
133: 
134: The same read kills the unbuilt joints that are true on this state: `flag || (atapi_st != NULL)`, `flag || (atapi_received != 0)`, `flag || (data_in != NULL)`, `flag || (data_in_len != 0)`, and the pure replacements `if (atapi_received)`, `if (data_in != NULL)`, `if (data_in_len)`. It does not kill `flag || (atapi_len != 0)` (length is 0), `D2_and_st` (flag is 0), or `D2_phase` (phase is 0).
135: 
136: Why it cannot redden an unchanged row. No existing fixture or oracle is edited. HEAD is green, as above. `D2_phase`, `D3_ge0`, `D7_lt1` and `D8_lt2` match HEAD on every reachable state. Width 2: `cons_eq2`, `bepop_ge4` and `lepop_ge4` compute the same pop count and the same consumed as HEAD. `D0_nocmd`, `D6_head`, `D7_latch`, `D8else_latch` and `cmdlen_ms10` do not run on an enabled data read with no dispatch. The sample is not a sector boundary, so it does not share the shipped PIO row's want-irq-1 oracle.
137: 
138: **Row 2. `stale count, PIO boundary irq`** (29 characters).
139: 
140: Fixture. The body of `row_pio_sector_irq` (`REPAIR3.c.txt:7055-7068`): `rep_fresh`, 1024 bytes queued, 255 width-2 reads, sample `a255`, the 256th width-2 read, sample `a256`, `occ`. Before the queue, synthesise the abandoned transfer: `atapi_st = scsi_transfer_alloc()`; `data_in` one non-NULL byte; `data_in_len = 1000`; `atapi_len = 1000`; `atapi_received = 0`; flag 0; phase 0. `rep_free` already frees `data_in` (`REPAIR3.c.txt:6501-6504`).
141: 
142: Oracle, copied and not loosened: `a255 == 0 && a256 == 1 && occ == 512 && warned_null_xfer == 0`.
143: 
144: HEAD. Flag 0, so `atapi_len` and `atapi_st` are ignored. The path is the shipped PIO row, which is measured green (`STATUS.md:7-8`). After 255 width-2 reads, 510 bytes have left a 1024-byte ring and the boundary interrupt has not fired. The 256th read leaves 512 bytes at a 512-byte boundary and `dev_wdc.c:1472-1476` sets `int_assert`. Green.
145: 
146: Kills `D2_st`, `D2_or_st`, and the unbuilt `if (atapi_len)` / `flag || (atapi_len != 0)` / `flag || (atapi_received < data_in_len)`. The mutant stays in the ATAPI arm. Each width-2 read consumes 2 (`dev_wdc.c:1255`; 2 < 1000, so the clamp does not shrink it). After 256 reads `atapi_len` is 488, so `dev_wdc.c:1318` stays false and `int_assert` stays 0. `a256 == 0`. Red. Length 1000 is the point of the row: it stays strictly above the 512 bytes these reads consume, so the kill is the missing boundary interrupt and not an early completion.
147: 
148: CHANNEL: `a256` (1 versus 0). `a255` is 0 on both. Occupancy is 512 on both (pops precede the flag). The latch is 0 on both.
149: 
150: Why it cannot redden an unchanged row. The shipped PIO row is not edited, so its oracle is still applied to a fresh device (flag 0, length 0, `atapi_st` NULL). On this new row HEAD takes that same PIO path. `D2_and_st` sees flag 0 and takes PIO, so `a256` stays 1. `D2_phase` sees phase 0 and takes PIO. The equivalents in section 2 match HEAD. Width 2 matches for `cons_eq2` and both pop mutants. No dispatch runs, so `D6_head`, `D7_latch`, `D8else_latch` and `cmdlen_ms10` stay on HEAD's path.
151: 
152: The cell is reachable without synthesis: a DATA-IN arm stores `atapi_len` at `dev_wdc.c:1793` while the flag is 1, and the next `wdc_command` clears the flag at `dev_wdc.c:917` and does not clear `atapi_len`. The fixture synthesises those fields, which is the idiom `row_pio_sector_irq` already uses for the queue. Driving it through IDENTIFY would also flush the ring at `dev_wdc.c:923` and replace the 1024-byte preload, and the copied oracle would no longer be the one the shipped row measured.
