168: `consumed` (`len == 4`, `len >= 2` at `dev_wdc.c:1255`) is a derived input. The six first-re-arm walks read one width each: 2, 4, 2, 1, 2, 2. Width 1 is the 1-byte write-route walk only. Width 2 is not read by the 4-byte walk or the 1-byte walk. Width 8 is read by none. SAMPLED. (e), named: `n_C6` (`writeflag == MEM_READ && len != 8`); `n_E_cons_ge4` (`len == 4` to `len >= 4`), which also changes widths 5, 6 and 7, not only 8 (`codex_r3pass_records.txt:72`); `n_F_VD1_width1_consumes2` (the width-1 accounting cell; the reading seat disputes this one, open question 5); `cons_eq2` (`len >= 2` to `len == 2` at `dev_wdc.c:1255`; widths 3 and 5-8 consume 1); `bepop_ge4` (`len == 4` to `len >= 4` at `dev_wdc.c:1210`); `lepop_ge4` (the same at `dev_wdc.c:1203`). The body buckets every width other than 1 and 4 with width 2 (`dev_wdc.c:1232-1235`, `:1255`). The least move of `>= 2` is `>= 3`, killed at width 2 (`n_E_cons_ge3`, 21/159, `TABLE3.txt:334`). The pop edits do not change `consumed`; a completion oracle does not kill them.
