90: **(e) DISCLOSED -- AGREE, with the body test named as NOT distinguishing the cell:** p2_G6, p2_G14, v_W5, v_V5 (the guard's partition is {CMDOUT, not CMDOUT}; it does not test DATAOUT, (LE,1)-in-DATAIN or width 8 as a case); v_V2 (no test on `atapi_len == 4096`); v_V6 (the 4-byte accept lanes sit outside the edited sites; constant fill); n_C6 and n_E_cons_ge4: the ternary at :1255 BUCKETS width 8 with width 2 (`len >= 2`), so the width-8 cell is not a case the body distinguishes -- agree; n_E_norm_le0 / ltm1 / deleted: the loop is data flow into `inbuf_len`, which the body tests `== 12` -- the (e) grade stands under the 2026-10-09 derived-input ruling, but the disclosure's REASON is a wrong record (Q4).
91: 
92: **(e) DISPUTED -- n_F_VD1_width1_consumes2 (`consumed = len == 4 ? 4 : 2`).**  The question as the brief puts it: name the body test that tests that cell as a case.  It is the SAME line that settles C6 the other way: `int consumed = len == 4 ? 4 : (len >= 2 ? 2 : 1);` (:1255).  `len >= 2` distinguishes width 1 as its own case exactly as it fails to distinguish width 8.  `len` is a function INPUT the guarded body reads directly, not a derived local; the 2026-10-09 ruling grades a DERIVED local (`consumed`) by the tests on IT, and does not demote the tests the body performs on the input that computes it.  Under the JOINT rule ("the PARTITION the body's own tests induce") the body-induced partition on `len` is {<2, >=2 and !=4, 4}; under the committed generating-set clause ("every idata bit the guarded body TESTS is written both clear and set" -- for a relational compare, its regions) the region below `len >= 2`'s bound must be driven at a floor cell WITH A CHANNEL; and the CHANNEL line says the one width-1 row (`write route: 1-byte store is refused`, 32768 width-1 reads at :7160-7161) is vacuous for this mutant -- it reads occupancy and a constant 0xc3 fill, which VD1 cannot move, as the contract itself records.  So the cell is driven and unobserved: a floor cell with no live row, not a disclosure.  Guest-visible consequence (REASONED): a driver reading an N-byte DATA-IN with byte-wide loads (legal on every CPU here) sees `atapi_len` fall by 2 per byte -- after N/2 reads the command COMPLETES with N/2 bytes still queued (ireason 0x03, count 0, an interrupt), and each further byte read re-runs the completion and re-asserts the interrupt.  First distinguishing input: arm any DATA-IN (INQUIRY, 36 bytes), ONE 8-bit DATA read, then read CYL_LO: HEAD 35 (0x23), VD1 34 (0x22).  Row owed (one line, L2 if the grade is upheld, L1 otherwise): in `write_route_case(..., 1, ...)` read CYL_LO/CYL_HI after the first 1-byte read and want 32767 -- or a two-call leg: arm 4 bytes, on